  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the Edges of Sleep shows clear scholarly uptake for a 2022 monograph, with 11 citations in OpenAlex and field-normalized standing in the top 10% for its year. The citation record is still modest in absolute terms, but it already shows the book entering academic conversations across film, media, aesthetics, feminist theory, and related humanities fields.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Jean Ma\u2019s At the Edges of Sleep, published by University of California Press in 2022, has begun to establish a discernible research footprint. The book\u2019s 11 OpenAlex citations and top-10%-for-year percentile indicate measurable scholarly attention, especially for a recent humanities title. The record suggests early but meaningful uptake rather than broad saturation, with citations appearing in peer-reviewed articles rather than being concentrated in a single venue or subfield.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape is notably interdisciplinary. Current citations connect the book to Critical Inquiry, Feminist Theory, and New Review of Film and Television Studies, pointing to resonance in film studies, critical theory, and feminist scholarship. The citing works also span concepts such as cinema and media studies, sound studies, posthumanist ethics, Japanese history and culture, and Asian culture and media studies, which aligns with the book\u2019s focus on sleep as both an aesthetic and conceptual problem in moving-image art.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Available readership and dissemination signals are consistent with a work that is accessible and circulating within research collections. OpenAlex indicates open access availability, including repository instances, and WorldCat/Open Library holdings are present. The record also notes a LibraryThing recognition as an ACLS Open Access Book Prize &amp; Arcadia Open Access Publishing Award finalist in 2026 for Literary/Media Studies, a sign of reader and community visibility beyond citation tracking.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At present, the evidence base is strongest on scholarly uptake and library reach. There is no event archive to summarize for public discourse, and downloads are not available in the digest, so those channels cannot be assessed here. Overall, the book appears to be an emerging reference point for discussions of sleep, attention, and embodiment in contemporary film and visual culture, with its influence already visible in humanities scholarship and likely to grow as the citation record matures.</p>",
